by M. Chan, A. Jensen, and E. Rubei.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe 4x4 minors of a 5xn matrix are a tropical basis (https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.5264)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.9 years ago; Authors: Melody Chan, Anders N. Jensen, Elena Rubei ... We then prove that the Kapranov rank of every 5xn matrix equals its tropical rank; equivalently, that the 4x4 minors of a 5xn matrix of variables form a tropical basis.\nTitle: The 4x4 minors of a 5xn matrix are a tropical basis\nAuthors: Melody Chan, Anders N. Jensen, Elena Rubei\nDate: Tue Dec 29 06:53:12 2009\n\nWe compute the space of 5x5 matrices of tropical rank at most 3 and show that it coincides with the space of 5x5 matrices of Kapranov rank at most 3, that is, the space of five labeled coplanar points in TP4. We then prove that the Kapranov rank of every 5xn matrix equals its tropical rank; equivalently, that the 4x4 minors of a 5xn matrix of variables form a tropical basis. This answers a question asked by Develin, Santos, and Sturmfels.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRESEARCH STATEMENT, MELODY CHAN (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=5c0b2d486dbf1c85567b719b7666ebe9e7cc2765&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; MELODY CHAN ... my joint publication with Anders Jensen and Elena Rubei, we obtained the following result:\nTheorem 17. [CJR09] The 4 × 4 minors of a 5 × n matrix form a tropical basis. ... Shitov subsequently solved\ncalled a tropical basis. Hence tropical bases are central to the computational study of tropical\nvarieties. Determinantal varieties, deﬁned by the r × r minors of a matrix of indeterminates\nand comprising matrices of rank at most r −1, constitute an excellent test case. In 2005,\nDevelin, Santos, and Sturmfels posed the question: for which r, d, n do the r × r minors of a\nd × n matrix form a tropical basis? They